        "abstract": "The use of current-mode preamplifiers in optical communications systems has been shown to offer significant bandwidth advantages over traditional voltage-mode designs. High input-impedance common-source preamplifiers typically have a limited bandwidth of the transistor f/sub T/. Low input-impedance common-gate preamplifiers allow this limit to be increased to better than f/sub T//4. A front-end stage based on GaAs current mirrors increases the bandwidth to approximately one-third of the transistor f/sub T/.",
